Part One
Cooperation Article 22 In carrying out the handling of child victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 18, the Regional Government may cooperate with: a. vertical agencies;
b. provincial government;
c. other district/city governments;
d. village/sub-district government;
e. non-governmental organizations;
f. private parties;
g. community; and/or
h. family.
Article 23 (1) In certain cases the Regional Government may cooperate with: a. other Regional Governments;
b. police;
c. advocates;
d. health workers;
e. psychologists;
f. social workers;
g. volunteer companions;
h. spiritual advisors;
i. other law enforcers, to assist Victims in the court proceedings;
j. National Commission on Violence Against Women;
k. Indonesian Child Protection Commission or Child Protection Institution; and/or l. certain parties desired for the benefit of the victim.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. socialization;
c. provision of legal aid;
d. health services;
e. psychological/psychologist services;
f. victim assistance;
g. counseling;
h. social rehabilitation;
i. spiritual guidance; and/or j. repatriation and social reintegration.

Part Two
Special Protection for Children Article 19 (1) Special protection for children is given to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. children who are victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. children who are victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. children who are victims of sexual crimes;
k. children who are victims of terrorist networks;
l. children with disabilities;
m. children who are victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
(2) Regional Government in implementing the handling of special protection for children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 10
(1) Health services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph - (1) letter b, are implemented using health facilities owned by the Regional Government or other referral hospitals by providing treatment services and health recovery for victims.
(2) Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by health workers for victims in accordance with professional standards, operational procedure standards and the medical needs of victims.
(3) Health services as referred to in paragraph (1), through efforts: a. anamnesis of victims;
b. examination of victims;
c. providing first aid;
d. health recovery both physical and psychological, carrying out efforts to prevent disease and other health disorders;
e. counseling;
f. referring to more adequate health service facilities if necessary can refer to other parties with a multidisciplinary approach, including health services, medicolegal, psychosocial assistance and legal aid; and/or
g. providing information to the police.
(4) In addition to the efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) for certain cases, health workers may provide reproductive health services in accordance with medical needs.
(5) In providing services as referred to in paragraph (3) and paragraph (4), health workers must make medical records in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations, (6) For every medical action to be carried out by health workers there must be consent for medical action from the victim or their family in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(7) For investigation purposes, authorized health workers must make a visum et repertum and/or visum et repertum psichiatricum. or make a medical certificate.
(8) Health workers have the right to obtain legal protection.
Article11 Legal aid and protection services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (1) letter g include:
a. providing protection in Safe Houses;
b. providing legal information to Victims;
c. providing Assistance for Victims as witnesses from the investigation process until the verdict;
d. providing special legal protection for child victims of violence can be carried out by appointing guardianship in accordance with laws and regulations.
Article12 (1) Repatriation and social reintegration as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (1) letter g are implemented with the following principles:
a. voluntary;
b. safe and dignified; and
c. respect for rights.
(2) In the event that the Regional Government is not yet able to implement repatriation and social reintegration as referred to in paragraph (1) may request assistance from related parties.
(3) Social services to administer social welfare for Children in conflict with the law are carried out by the Regional Government together with LPKS with other social institutions.
(4) LPKS and other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (3) are rehabilitation and temporary shelter places for children in conflict with the law before repatriation and social reintegration are implemented.

Part Three
Integrated Services for Victims of Violence Article 8 (1) The Regional Government implements integrated services for Victims of Violence, through activities:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. Counseling;
d. psychological services;
e. spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation services;
g. legal aid and assistance services;
h. safe house services; and/or
i. repatriation and social reintegration.
(2) In the event that the Regional Government does not yet have counseling personnel as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, it may request assistance from social workers, volunteer companions, and/or spiritual guides.
(3) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within the police force;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and Safe Houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for the recovery of victims including perpetrators.
(4) The provision of services to Victims of Violence is carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government together with other social institutions. (5) Other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (4) may receive and send case referrals from other service units in a networked manner. (6) Integrated Services for victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) through paragraph (5) are implemented in accordance with standard operating procedures.
(7) Provisions regarding standard operating procedures as referred to in paragraph (6) are regulated in a Regent Regulation.
Article 9 (1) Complaint services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (1) letter a are implemented to receive reports of acts of violence.
(2) Reports as referred to in paragraph (1) may originate from: a. victims;
b. referrals; or
c. outreach.

Second Part
Prevention of Acts of Violence Article 16 (1) The Regional Government conducts prevention of acts of violence against women by means of:
a. socializing laws and regulations;
b. conducting empowerment and awareness raising to families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling; c. conducting efforts to increase the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
d. conducting efforts to build community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from violence in an integrated community-based manner;
e. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities, religious institutions/organizations and various non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent violence against women and children;
f. establishing a violence prevention system, mapping of locations or areas prone to violence;
g. increasing community awareness that respects women and protects children;
h. establishing UPTD PPA in every Sub-district, and Women and Children Protection Service Posts at the Village and Sub-district levels; and
i. establishing a women and children protection task force.
(2) In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. the community;
c. vertical agencies;
d. law enforcement apparatus;
e. educational institutions;
f. health institutions;
g. religious institutions/organizations;
h. social community institutions;
i. the business sector; and
j. other institutions.
(3) Prevention of acts of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted in an integrated manner and coordinated by the Regional Apparatus that has the main duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

